  "major": "american-sign-language-psychology-bs-940a42f2",
  "major_name": "American Sign Language and Psychology",
  "degree": "BS",
  "campus": "Boston",
  "college": "College of Social Sciences and Humanities",
  "overview_text": "American Sign Language is estimated to be the third most used language in the United States and its visibility in society is on the rise in recent years. An estimated 1 to 2 million Deaf, DeafBlind, or hard of hearing people in the United States use ASL as their primary language, and a large number of non-Deaf signers use or learn ASL as a second language. There is a broad societal misunderstanding of Deaf, signing people’s minority language and culture—their lived experience—which creates barriers to society in many domains. As a result of being viewed from the medical model of disability, rather than the social model as a cultural-linguistic minority group, Deaf people experience socioeconomic disadvantage and language deprivation at the hands of those who profess to \"help.\" Meanwhile, there is much to be learned and appreciated about the psychology of the Deaf lived experience. We can learn about resilience as well as about language, behavior, and cognition that comes from experiencing the world through a visual language. It is also important to understand the detrimental effects that social isolation and language deprivation can have on Deaf people. While state and federal legislation enables bilingual education and interventions─as well as improved civic engagement by Deaf, hard of hearing, and DeafBlind individuals─there is a continued need to elevate quality of life, equity, and the rich contributions of the diverse members of the American Deaf communities. The program offers a wide array of courses as well as volunteer, co-op, and research opportunities to learn from Deaf people's lived experiences through community and industry connections. This combined major is designed to assist students in acquiring competence in ASL, develop an understanding of the values of diverse American Deaf communities and Deaf culture, as well as gain insights into various features of spoken language. Students experience firsthand the principles underlying the spatial organization of discourse required by signed languages. The curriculum integrates Deaf culture with cognitive psychology, psycholinguistics, and the structures of human languages in general and ASL in particular. This major provides a foundation for graduate studies of ASL, psychology and Deaf people, or interpreting in mental health contexts.",
  "source_text": "Complete all courses listed below unless otherwise indicated. Also complete any corequisite labs, recitations, clinicals, or tools courses where specified and complete any additional courses needed beyond specific college and major requirements to satisfy graduation credit requirements.\nUniversitywide Requirements\nAll undergraduate students are required to complete the\nUniversitywide Requirements\n.\nNUpath Requirements\nAll undergraduate students are required to complete the\nNUpath Requirements\n.\nAmerican Sign Language Requirements\nStudents wishing to do a co-op are required to demonstrate intermediate language proficiency by completing the first four AMSL courses before undertaking co-op.\nCourse List\nCode\nTitle\nHours\nAmerican Sign Language\nAMSL 1101\nElementary ASL 1\n4\nAMSL 1102\nElementary ASL 2\n4\nAMSL 2101\nIntermediate ASL 1\n4\nAMSL 2102\nIntermediate ASL 2\n4\nAMSL 3101\nAdvanced ASL 1\n4\nAMSL 3102\nAdvanced ASL 2\n4\nSocial and Cultural World\nDEAF 1500\nDeaf People in Society\n4\nDEAF 2500\nDeaf History and Culture\n4\nLinguistics\nDEAF 2700\nASL Linguistics\n4\nLING 1150\nIntroduction to Linguistics\n4\nInterpreting\nINTP 3500\nThe Interpreting Profession\n2\nINTP 3510\nInterpreting Inquiry Texts\n4\nPsychology Requirements\nCourse List\nCode\nTitle\nHours\nPsychology Core Courses\nPSYC 1101\nFoundations of Psychology\n4\nPSYC 2320\nStatistics in Psychological Research\n1\n4\nPSYC 3466\nCognition\n4\nComplete two of the following personal/social bases of behavior (Area A) courses:\n8\nPSYC 3400\nPersonality\nPSYC 3402\nSocial Psychology\nPSYC 3404\nDevelopmental Psychology\nPSYC 3406\nClinical Psychology and Mental Health\nPsychology Lab or Directed Study\nComplete one of the following (this requirement may not be fulfilled via transfer credit):\n4\nOne PSYC laboratory course numbered 4600-4649\nPSYC 4991\nDirected Study Research\nPsychology Electives\nComplete two PSYC courses, not counted elsewhere, numbered 2350-2399 or 3000+.\n8\n1\nStudents who receive transfer credit for the Advanced Placement Statistics exam may complete the 1-semester-hour  PSYC 2315 course (requires department permission).\nIntegrative Requirement\nCourse List\nCode\nTitle\nHours\nIntegrative Requirements\nINTP 3970\nResearch Methods\n4\nINTP 4940\nInterpreting Research Capstone\n4\nPSYC 3464\nPsychology of Language\n4\nSupporting Courses\nCourse List\nCode\nTitle\nHours\nIntroduction to College\nINTP 1000\nAmerican Sign Language at Northeastern\n1\nor\nINSC 1000\nScience at Northeastern\nExperiential Learning Introduction\nEESH 2000\nProfessional Development for Co-op\n1\nor\nEESC 2000\nProfessional Development for Co-op\nMathematics Requirement\nComplete one of the following\n4\nCS 1800\nDiscrete Structures\nMATH 1213\nInteractive Mathematics\nMATH 1215\nMathematical Thinking\nMATH 1231\nCalculus for Business and Economics\nMATH 1241\nCalculus 1\nMATH 1242\nCalculus 2\nMATH 1340\nIntensive Calculus for Engineers\nMATH 1341\nCalculus 1 for Science and Engineering\nMATH 1342\nCalculus 2 for Science and Engineering\nWriting Requirements\nENGW 1111\nFirst-Year Writing\n4\nor\nENGW 1102\nFirst-Year Writing for Multilingual Writers\nENGW 3307\nAdvanced Writing in the Sciences\n4\nor\nENGW 3315\nInterdisciplinary Advanced Writing in the Disciplines\nCombined Major GPA Requirement\nMinimum 2.750 GPA required in all AMSL, INTP,  and DEAF courses\nMinimum 2.000 required in all PSYC courses                                                                                                                                                Minimum 2.500 overall GPA required\nNUpath Requirements\nThe following NUpath requirements are met by the major:\nAnalyzing and Using Data (AD)\nDemonstrating Thought and Action in a Capstone (CE)\nEngaging Differences and Diversity (DD)\nEngaging with the Natural and Designed World (ND)\nInterpreting Culture (IC)\nUnderstanding Societies and Institutions (SI)\nWriting-Intensive (WI)\nOther NUpath requirements may be fulfilled by electives in the program.\nCombined Major Credit Requirement\nComplete 90 semester hours in the major.\nProgram Requirement\n128 total semester hours required",
